<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecclesiastes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ecclesiastes is a book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, part of its wisdom literature, presented as the reflections of Qoheleth, the Preacher, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, traditionally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. Its theme that all is vanity under the sun, balanced by counsel to enjoy life and fear God, is usually dated by scholars to the fourth or third century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecclesiastes.jpg" alt="Ecclesiastes" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gershonmk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecclesiastes_2-3_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
